Questions:
Title: Why is the (virtual) destructor of the derived class not called when deleting an array through a pointer to the base class?.
Body: <p>I have an <code>Animal</code> class with a virtual destructor, and a derived class <code>Cat</code>.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

struct Animal
{
    Animal() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Animal constructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
    virtual ~Animal() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Animal destructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
};

struct Cat : public Animal
{
    Cat() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Cat constructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
    ~Cat() override { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Cat destructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
};

int main()
{
    const Animal *j = new Cat[1];
    delete[] j;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This gives the output:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Animal constructor  <br />
Cat constructor  <br />
Animal destructor</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't understand why is the <code>Cat</code>'s destructor not called, when my base class destructor is virtual?</p>

Answers:
None. You can use the following code to answer your question:

```python
#include <iostream>

struct Animal
{
    Animal() { std::cout << "Animal constructor" << std::endl; }
    virtual ~Animal() { std::cout << "Animal destructor" << std::endl; }
};

struct Cat : public Animal
{
    Cat() { std::cout << "Cat constructor" << std::endl; }
    ~Cat() override { std::cout << "Cat destructor" << std::endl; }
};

int main()
{
    const Animal *j = new Cat[1];
    delete[] j;
}
```

The reason why the destructor of the derived class (`Cat`) is not called when deleting an array through a pointer to the base class (`Animal`) is due to the way the `delete` operator works in C++. When you use `delete` to deallocate memory allocated by `new`, the compiler looks at the type of the pointer and calls the destructor for the object pointed to. In this case, the pointer is of type `Animal`, so the destructor of `Animal` is called, which only deletes the `Animal` part of the object, not the `Cat` part.

To call the destructor of the derived class, you need to use the `delete` operator with a pointer of the same type as the object you allocated with `new`. In this case, you can use `delete[]` with a pointer of type `Cat*` to call the destructor of `Cat` as well. Here's an updated version of the code:

```python
#include <iostream>

struct Animal
{
    Animal() { std::cout << "Animal constructor" << std::endl; }
    virtual ~Animal() { std::cout << "Animal destructor" << std::endl; }
};

struct Cat : public Animal
{
    Cat() { std::cout << "Cat constructor" << std::endl; }
    ~Cat() override { std::cout << "Cat destructor" << std::endl; }
};

int main()
{
    const Animal *j = new Cat[1];
    delete[] static_cast<Cat*>(j);
}
```

In this updated code, we use `static_cast` to cast the pointer `j` to a pointer of type `Cat*`, so that the destructor of `Cat` is called when we use `delete[]` on it.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.<|fim_middle|>

To make your code more readable and maintainable, you can use `std::array` directly with a nested template. This approach will allow you to define a 2D array without using a macro.

Here's how you can do it:

```cpp
#include <array>

// Define the 2D array using std::array
std::array<std::array<int, YSIZE>, XSIZE> bar;

// You can also initialize the array in the same line if needed
std::array<std::array<int, YSIZE>, XSIZE> bar = {};
```

This approach avoids the need for a macro and makes the code more readable and easier to understand. You can use this approach for 3D arrays as well:

```cpp
std::array<std::array<std::array<int, ZSIZE>, YSIZE>, XSIZE> bar;
```

This will give you a 3D array where each element is of type `int`. You can initialize it similarly to the 2D array.

This way, you can avoid the macro hack and maintain cleaner and more readable code.

Questions:
Title: Are multidimensional array accesses sequenced?.
Body: <p>Take the following:</p>
<pre><code>int a(void) {
    puts(&quot;a&quot;);
    return 0;
}

int b(void) {
    puts(&quot;b&quot;);
    return 1;
}

int c(void) {
    puts(&quot;c&quot;);
    return 2;
}

int d(void) {
    puts(&quot;d&quot;);
    return 3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Will the following have predictable behavior?</p>
<pre><code>int arr[4][4][4][4];
arr[a()][b()][c()][d()] = 1;
</code></pre>
<p>Is it guaranteed to print in this order:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>a
b
c
d
</code></pre>
<p>I am aware that constructs such as the following are invalid:</p>
<pre><code>int i;
i = i++;
</code></pre>
<p>This is because <code>=</code> is an unsequenced operator, so whether <code>i</code> or <code>i++</code> is evaluated first is undefined. It is undefined behavior to access and modify a single object before another sequence point.</p>
<p>Put another way, is the following valid:</p>
<pre><code>int i = 0, arr[4][4][4][4];
arr[i++][i++][i++][i++] = 1;
</code></pre>
<p>Or does it invoke undefined behavior due to unsequenced modification and access to <code>i</code>?</p>
<p>According to the C standard, is there a defined sequence point between each successive <code>[]</code> while indexing a multidimensional array?</p>
<p>To be clear, neither of these examples have to do with precedence, the placement of implicit parenthesis, the order in which the operators operate on their operands. The question is about sequencing, the order in which the <em>operands themselves</em> are evaluated.</p>

Title: std::sort invocation causes subscript out of range compile-time error.
Body: <p>The below code fails to compile with error: &quot;array subscript 16 is outside array bounds of …&quot;.
I don't understand why, <code>w</code> is guaranteed to be &lt;= arr1.size().</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/5n5KohsnW" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/5n5KohsnW</a></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &lt;tuple&gt;

extern bool test_func();

int tempfunc() 
{
    std::array&lt;std::tuple&lt;double,int&gt;,5&gt; arr1;
    int w = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i ) {
        if( test_func()) {
            arr1[w] = {3.,4};
            w++;
        }
    }
    std::sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.begin() + w);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
To fix this issue, you can modify your code to ensure that the `w` index is within the bounds of the `arr1` array. Here's an updated version of your code:

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <algorithm>
#include <tuple>

extern bool test_func();

int tempfunc() 
{
    std::array&lt;std::tuple&lt;double,int&gt;,5&gt; arr1;
    int w = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i ) {
        if( test_func()) {
            arr1[w] = {3.,4};
            w++;
        }
    }

    // Ensure that w is within the bounds of arr1
    if (w &gt;= arr1.size()) {
        w = arr1.size();
    }

    std::sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.begin() + w);

    return 0;
}
```

In the updated code, we added a check to ensure that `w` is within the bounds of the `arr1` array. If `w` is greater than or equal to the size of `arr1`, we set `w` to the size of `arr1`. This ensures that we don't attempt to access an out-of-bounds index when sorting the array. 

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.

