Title: thread_local + std::thread deadlock on destruction.
Body: <p>Anyone knows what kind of UB is this? The following code deadlocks on <code>jthread</code> destruction when built with MSVC 19.29.30148, sometimes it deadlocks after std::cout and sometimes before.
This is somehow related to thread_local, but I cannot see what is the issue. It seems to work fine under other compilers and platforms.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;thread&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

int main(void)
{
    std::thread t2(
        [&amp;] {
            thread_local std::jthread asd([] {
                    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;ASD&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
                    });
        });
    if (t2.joinable()) { t2.join(); }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Update: Reproducible both on static and dynamic runtime</p>

Answers:
This issue is related to the interaction between the `std::thread` and `thread_local` variables. The problem arises when the `std::jthread` is destroyed, and the `thread_local` variable is accessed from another thread. In this case, the destructor of `thread_local` variable is called, and it tries to join the `std::jthread` object. However, the `std::jthread` object is already destroyed, and the join operation causes a deadlock. This behavior is undefined behavior (UB) and can be platform-dependent.</p>
<p>To avoid this issue, you can use the `std::jthread` destructor to join the thread explicitly, or use the `std::jthread::join` method instead of the destructor. Here's an example of how to fix the issue:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;thread&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

int main(void)
{
    std::jthread t2(
        [&amp;] {
            thread_local std::jthread asd([] {
                    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;ASD&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
                    });
        });
    if (t2.joinable()) { t2.join(); }
}
</code></pre>
<p>By using the `std::jthread::join` method, the `std::jthread` object is joined explicitly, and the deadlock issue is avoided.</p>
<p>It's also worth mentioning that the issue can occur on other platforms and compilers, but it's specific to the interaction between `std::thread`, `thread_local`, and the `std::jthread` destructor.</p>
