Answers:
To remove the background of list items when dragging (and when long-pressing) with onMove in SwiftUI, you can use the <code>.contentShape()</code> modifier to override the default background of the item. Here's an example:
<pre><code>@State var items = [&quot;item0&quot;, &quot;item1&quot;, &quot;item2&quot;]
List {
    ForEach(items, id: \.self) { item in
        ZStack {
            Rectangle()
                .frame(width: 150, height: 65)
                .contentShape(Rectangle()) // Add this line
            Text(item)
        }
        .onMove { from, to in
            items.move(fromOffsets: from, toOffset: to)
        }
    }
    .listRowSeparator(.hidden)
    .listRowBackground(Color.clear)
}
.listStyle(.plain)
.scrollContentBackground(.hidden)
</code></pre>
This will override the default background of the item and make it transparent, so you won't see the background while dragging or long-holding the item.

However, this won't remove the background when you start dragging. To remove the background while actively dragging, you can use the <code>.onDrag {} \@ViewBuilder preview: {}</code> modifier. Here's an example:
<pre><code>@State var items = [&quot;item0&quot;, &quot;item1&quot;, &quot;item2&quot;]
List {
    ForEach(items, id: \.self) { item in
        ZStack {
            Rectangle()
                .frame(width: 150, height: 65)
                .contentShape(Rectangle())
            Text(item)
        }
        .onMove { from, to in
            items.move(fromOffsets: from, toOffset: to)
        }
        .onDrag {} \@ViewBuilder preview: {
            Rectangle()
                .frame(width: 150, height: 65)
        }
    }
    .listRowSeparator(.hidden)
    .listRowBackground(Color.clear)
}
.listStyle(.plain)
.scrollContentBackground(.hidden)
</code></pre>
This will remove the background while actively dragging, but it will still appear when we long-hold the item, right before we start dragging. If you want to remove the background when long-holding the item as well, you can use the <code>.onLongPressGesture(minimumDuration: 0.5) {}</code> modifier. Here's an example:
<pre><code>@State var items = [&quot;item0&quot;, &quot;item1&quot;, &quot;item2&quot;]
List {
    ForEach(items, id: \.self) { item in
        ZStack {
            Rectangle()
                .frame(width: 150, height: 65)
                .contentShape(Rectangle())
            Text(item)
        }
        .onMove { from, to in
            items.move(fromOffsets: from, toOffset: to)
        }
        .onDrag {} \@ViewBuilder preview: {
            Rectangle()
                .frame(width: 150, height: 65)
        }
        .onLongPressGesture(minimumDuration: 0.5) {
            // Do nothing
        }
    }
    .listRowSeparator(.hidden)
    .listRowBackground(Color.clear)
}
.listStyle(.plain)
.scrollContentBackground(.hidden)
</code></pre>
This will remove the background when long-holding the item as well.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions or if you need further assistance.

Here's a solution that should work for both versions of the problem:
<pre><code>x &lt;- list(id = c(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;a&quot;), key = c(&quot;foo&quot;, &quot;foo&quot;, &quot;bar&quot;), value = c(1, 2, 3))

# Create a list of lists
y &lt;- list()
for (i in 1:length(x$id)) {
  if (!x$id[i] %in% names(y)) {
    y[[x$id[i]]] &lt;- list()
  }
  y[[x$id[i]]][[x$key[i]]] &lt;- x$value[i]
}

# Convert the list of lists to a nested list
z &lt;- lapply(names(y), function(x) {
  lapply(names(y[[x]]), function(y) y[[x]]))
})

# Convert the nested list to a list of lists
z &lt;- lapply(names(z), function(x) {
  lapply(names(z[[x]]), function(y) z[[x]][[y]])
})

</code></pre>
This solution uses a for loop to iterate over the elements of the list, creating a new list of lists `y` where each element of `x$id` is a key in the list and each element of `x$key` is a subkey in the list. The value of each element of `x$value` is then assigned to the corresponding subkey.
The solution then converts the list of lists `y` to a nested list `z` using `lapply` and `names`. Finally, the nested list `z` is converted back to a list of lists using another `lapply` and `names` call.
I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions.

Questions:
Title: Thread safe read and instantiate a list.
Body: <p>I need to implement a list as the following:</p>
<pre><code>private List&lt;Foo&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

public synchronized Foo getFoo(Condition condition) {
    // loop the list and get a foo that meets the condition
    for (Foo foo: list) {
        if(condition.check(foo)) {
            return foo;
        }
    }
    return null;
}

public synchronized void refresh() {
    list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(fetchFromDB());
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is there better way to avoid synchronized method to make the read and instantiate thread safe? I think CopyOnWriteArrayList won't help here because there are no mutative operations on the same list and every time we just create a new list. Am I right?</p>
<hr />
<p>UPDATED: to clarify, the list will only be fully set by the refresh() method, there is no other mutation to the list.</p>

Answers:
None. To avoid synchronized method, you can use a thread-safe data structure such as ConcurrentHashMap or CopyOnWriteArrayList. However, in your case, since the list is only refreshed by the refresh() method and not mutated after that, using ConcurrentHashMap would be a better choice. Here's an example of how you can implement it:</p>
<pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

public class FooList {
    private ConcurrentMap&lt;Integer, Foo&gt; list = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();

    public Foo getFoo(Condition condition) {
        // loop the list and get a foo that meets the condition
        for (Foo foo: list.values()) {
            if(condition.check(foo)) {
                return foo;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    public void refresh() {
        list = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;(fetchFromDB());
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In this implementation, we use a ConcurrentHashMap instead of an ArrayList to store the list of Foo objects. This data structure is thread-safe and allows concurrent reads and writes. The refresh() method creates a new ConcurrentHashMap and assigns it to the list variable, which will automatically replace the old list with the new one. The getFoo() method iterates over the values in the ConcurrentHashMap and returns the first Foo object that meets the condition.</p>
<p>Using ConcurrentHashMap ensures that the read and instantiate operations are thread-safe and avoids the need for synchronized methods.</p><|fim_middle|>
