Questions:
Title: simple way te to compute rgb buffer from I420 frame ffmpeg.
Body: <p>I'm trying to compute directly rgb buffer from avframe. Something is going wrong since the obtained image is wrong. Extracting grey image from AVFrame-&gt;data[0] is working fine. However I'm not able to extract colored image</p>
<pre><code>inline void YCrCb_to_RGB8(int Y, int Cr, int Cb, int&amp; R, int&amp; G, int&amp; B){

   R = (int)(Y + 1.402 *(Cr - 128));
   G = (int)(Y - 0.344136*(Cb-128) -0.71414*(Cr-128));
   B = (int)(Y + 1.772 *(Cb-128));

   if (R &lt; 0) R = 0; else if (R &gt; 255) R = 255;
   if (G &lt; 0) G = 0; else if (G &gt; 255) G = 255;
   if (B &lt; 0) B = 0; else if (B &gt; 255) B = 255;
}

int getRGB8buffer(AVFrame* pFrame, byte* buffer){

   const int width = pFrame-&gt;width, height = pFrame-&gt;height;
   int Y, Cr, Cb;
   int R, G, B;

   int pixel = 0;
   for (int y = 0; y &lt; height; y++)
   {
    for (int x = 0; x &lt; width; x++)
    {

        Y = pFrame-&gt;data[0][x + y * width];
        Cr = pFrame-&gt;data[1][x / 2 + ((int)(y / 2)) * pFrame-&gt;linesize[1]];
        Cb = pFrame-&gt;data[2][x / 2 + ((int)(y / 2)) * pFrame-&gt;linesize[2]];

        YCrCb_to_RGB8(Y, Cr, Cb, R, G, B);
        buffer[pixel * 3 + 0] = R;
        buffer[pixel * 3 + 1] = G;
        buffer[pixel * 3 + 2] = B;
        pixel++;
    }
   }
   return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>When I save the obtained image as ppm using</p>
<pre><code>int save_RGB_frame(unsigned char* buf, int wrap, int xsize,int ysize, const char* filename){

   FILE* f;
   int i;
   f = fopen(filename, &quot;w&quot;);
   // portable ppm format -&gt; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netpbm#PPM_example
   fprintf(f, &quot;P6\n%d %d\n%d\n&quot;, xsize, ysize, 255);

   // writing line by line
   for (i = 0; i &lt; ysize; i++)
      fwrite(buf + i * wrap, 1, xsize*3, f);
   fclose(f);

   return 0;
 }
</code></pre>
<p>The resulting image is wrong
link the the resulting image <a href="https://github.com/hacenesh/ffmpeg_question/blob/main/img_2028144.ppm" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/hacenesh/ffmpeg_question/blob/main/img_2028144.ppm</a></p>

Title: React Native: Cannot position Image with resizeMode &quot;contain&quot; at the flex-end of parent component.
Body: <p>So I'm trying to animate two images by sliding them out of the screen in opposite directions in React Native. The 2 images are positioned side by side, <strong>with each image's parent component taking up 50% of the screen width</strong>. I want the images to be positioned next to each other without any gap between them, AND they also have to use <code>resizeMode=&quot;contain&quot;</code> to ensure that the images <strong>scale proportionally</strong> to fit within their respective containers, <strong>while preserving their aspect ratios</strong>.</p>
<p>I have tried everything that I can think of to position the left-hand Image component to the very end of its parent component, and the right-hand Image to the very start of its parent component so that they would touch each other but nothing seems to work. The images are always positioned at the center of their respective containers as shown in the image bellow.</p>
<ol>
<li>On the <strong>left side</strong> I have the left half of the image which I want to position at the very end of its <strong>blue container</strong>.</li>
<li>On the <strong>right side</strong> I have the right half of the image which I want to position at the very start of its <strong>red container</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/HJuxk.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/HJuxk.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Here is my JSX code:</p>
<pre><code>return (
      &lt;View
        style={{
          flexDirection: 'row',
        }}
      &gt;
        &lt;Animated.View
          style={{
            width: width / 2,
            height: '100%',
            justifyContent: 'flex-end',
            alignItems: 'flex-end',
            transform: [{ translateX: leftTranslateX }],
            backgroundColor: 'blue',
          }}
        &gt;
          &lt;Image
            source={leftSplash}
            style={{ width: '100%', height: '100%', alignSelf: 'flex-end' }}
            resizeMode=&quot;contain&quot;
          /&gt;
        &lt;/Animated.View&gt;
        &lt;Animated.View
          style={{
            width: width / 2,
            height: '100%',
            justifyContent: 'flex-start',
            alignItems: 'flex-start',
            transform: [{ translateX: rightTranslateX }],
            backgroundColor: 'red',
          }}
        &gt;
          &lt;Image
            source={rightSplash}
            style={{ width: '100%', height: '100%', alignSelf: 'flex-start' }}
            resizeMode=&quot;contain&quot;
          /&gt;
        &lt;/Animated.View&gt;
      &lt;/View&gt;
  );
</code></pre>

Answers:
Certainly! Let's break down the problem and find a solution step by step.

### Problem Breakdown
1. **Positioning Images**: We need to position two images side by side within their respective containers.
2. **Contain Mode**: Images should scale proportionally within their containers while preserving their aspect ratios.
3. **Flex End and Flex Start**: The left image should be at the end of its container, and the right image should be at the start of its container.

### Solution Approach
To achieve this, we can use Flexbox properties along with `align-self` for the images. Here’s how we can do it:

1. **Use Flexbox**: Set the parent view to `flex: 1` and use `justify-content: space-between` to distribute the children evenly.
2. **Align Self**: Use `align-self: flex-end` for the left image and `align-self: flex-start` for the right image.

### Updated Code
Here’s the updated JSX code:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { View, Image, Animated } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const leftTranslateX = new Animated.Value(0);
  const rightTranslateX = new Animated.Value(0);

  // Example images
  const leftSplash = require('./leftSplash.png');
  const rightSplash = require('./rightSplash.png');

  return (
    <View
      style={{
        flex: 1,
        flexDirection: 'row',
        justifyContent: 'space-between', // Distribute children evenly
        alignItems: 'stretch', // Stretch children to fill available space
      }}
    >
      <Animated.View
        style={{
          flex: 1,
          justifyContent: 'flex-end',
          backgroundColor: 'blue',
        }}
      >
        <Image
          source={leftSplash}
          style={{ flex: 1, resizeMode: 'contain' }}
        />
      </Animated.View>
      <Animated.View
        style={{
          flex: 1,
          justifyContent: 'flex-start',
          backgroundColor: 'red',
        }}
      >
        <Image
          source={rightSplash}
          style={{ flex: 1, resizeMode: 'contain' }}
        />
      </Animated.View>
    </View>
  );
};

export default App;
```

### Explanation
1. **Flex Direction**: Set `flexDirection: 'row'` to arrange children horizontally.
2. **Justify Content**: Use `justifyContent: 'space-between'` to distribute the children evenly along the main axis.
3. **Align Items**: Use `alignItems: 'stretch'` to ensure that the children stretch to fill the available space.
4. **Flex**: Set `flex: 1` for each child to allow them to take up equal space.
5. **Align Self**: Use `alignSelf: 'flex-end'` for the left image and `alignSelf: 'flex-start'` for the right image.

This setup ensures that the images are positioned side by side without any gaps and that they scale proportionally while preserving their aspect ratios.

Questions:
Title: Why do @fs occur in the image path I import?.
Body: <p>I create a script named &quot;FileUtil.jsx&quot; to import a bunch of images and export them to another component.</p>
<pre><code>import Oolong from '../assests/img/Oolong.png'
import ChaiTea from '../assests/img/ChaiTea.png'
import CharcoalRoastedOolong from '../assests/img/CharcoalRoastedOolong.png'
import HighMountainOolong from '../assests/img/HighMountainOolong.png'
import RubyBlackTea from '../assests/img/RubyBlackTea.png'

export const images={
    &quot;Oolong&quot;:Oolong,
    &quot;Chai Tea&quot;:ChaiTea,
    &quot;Charcoal Roasted Oolong&quot;:CharcoalRoastedOolong,
    &quot;High Mountain Oolong&quot;:HighMountainOolong,
    &quot;Ruby Black Tea&quot;:RubyBlackTea,
};
</code></pre>
<p>Another script named &quot;OrderPage.jsx&quot; which import images</p>
<pre><code>import {images} from './FileUtil';
console.log(images['Oolong']);
console.log(images['Chai Tea']);
console.log(images['Charcoal Roasted Oolong']);
console.log(images['High Mountain Oolong']);
console.log(images['Ruby Black Tea']);
</code></pre>
<p>And I only got @fs occur in my first image path which is Oolong
@fs error:
<a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/A3ADK.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer" title="Oolong @fs error"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/A3ADK.png" alt="Oolong @fs error" title="Oolong @fs error" /></a></p>
<p>After checking everything in my code, I still have no idea why this occurs.
However, I could just remove @fs from my path after importing images, and everything works normally.</p>
<pre><code>images['Oolong']=images['Oolong'].replace(&quot;/@fs&quot;,&quot;&quot;); //in OrderPage.jsx
</code></pre>
<p>But, I still want to know why this happened. Could anyone explain this?</p>

Answers:
Certainly! Let's dive into why you're seeing the `@fs` prefix in your image paths when you import them.

### Understanding `@fs`

The `@fs` prefix in file paths typically indicates that the path is relative to the file system rather than the current working directory. This can happen if your project structure or build process involves certain configurations.

### Possible Causes and Solutions

1. **Project Structure**:
   - Ensure that your project structure is correctly set up. If your images are located in a subdirectory, make sure the path reflects that. For example:
     ```javascript
     import Oolong from '../assests/img/Oolong.png';
     ```

2. **Build Process**:
   - If you’re using a build process like Webpack or Vite, it might be adding the `@fs` prefix for some reason. Check your build configuration files (e.g., `webpack.config.js`, `vite.config.js`) for any settings related to file paths.

3. **File System Path**:
   - When you run your application, the file system path might differ from what you expect. For instance, if you’re running your application from a different directory, the paths will adjust accordingly. Use absolute paths if possible to avoid this issue.

### Example Fix

Here’s how you might handle the `@fs` prefix in your code:

#### In `FileUtil.jsx`:
```javascript
import Oolong from '../assests/img/Oolong.png';
import ChaiTea from '../assests/img/ChaiTea.png';
import CharcoalRoastedOolong from '../assests/img/CharcoalRoastedOolong.png';
import HighMountainOolong from '../assests/img/HighMountainOolong.png';
import RubyBlackTea from '../assests/img/RubyBlackTea.png';

export const images = {
    'Oolong': Oolong,
    'Chai Tea': ChaiTea,
    'Charcoal Roasted Oolong': CharcoalRoastedOolong,
    'High Mountain Oolong': HighMountainOolong,
    'Ruby Black Tea': RubyBlackTea,
};
```

#### In `OrderPage.jsx`:
```javascript
import { images } from './FileUtil';

console.log(images['Oolong']);
console.log(images['Chai Tea']);
console.log(images['Charcoal Roasted Oolong']);
console.log(images['High Mountain Oolong']);
console.log(images['Ruby Black Tea']);
```

### Additional Debugging Steps

1. **Print Path Before Import**:
   - Add console logs to print the path before importing to see if it includes the `@fs` prefix:
     ```javascript
     console.log('Oolong path:', Oolong);
     ```

2. **Check Build Configuration**:
   - Review your build configuration files to ensure there are no settings that add the `@fs` prefix.

3. **Absolute Paths**:
   - Use absolute paths to avoid any potential issues with relative paths:
     ```javascript
     import Oolong from '/path/to/assests/img/Oolong.png';
     ```

By following these steps, you should be able to identify and resolve the issue with the `@fs` prefix in your image paths.

