Answers:
You can merge the two AJAX functions into one by using a single route and a single form. You can use a hidden input field to determine which form is being submitted. Here's an example of how you can do this:

<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
function insertForm() {
  var form = document.getElementById('addEventForm');
  var formData = new FormData(form);
  formData.append('action', 'insertEvent');

  var customForms = document.querySelectorAll('form[name="addCustomField"]');
  customForms.forEach(function(customForm) {
    var customFormData = new FormData(customForm);
    formData.append('customFields', JSON.stringify(customFormData));
  });

  $.ajaxSetup({
    headers: {
      'X-CSRF-TOKEN': '{{ csrf_token() }}'
    }
  });

  $.ajax({
    type: 'POST',
    dataType: 'json',
    url: '{{ url(' / inputForm ') }}',
    data: formData,
    contentType: false,
    processData: false,
    success: function(response) {
      console.log(response);
      var successDiv = document.getElementById('successMessage');
      successDiv.innerHTML = response.message;
      successDiv.classList.add('alert', 'alert-success');
    }
  });
}
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
This function will handle both the single input form and the multiple custom fields form. The `action` parameter will be used to determine which form is being submitted. The `customFields` parameter will be used to send the custom fields data as a JSON string.
You can also use a single route to handle both forms by checking the `action` parameter and calling the appropriate function. Here's an example of how you can do this:

<pre><code>public function inputForm(Request $request)
{
    if ($request->has('action') && $request->input('action') === 'insertEvent') {
        // Handle single input form
    } else {
        // Handle multiple custom fields form
    }
}
</code></pre>
