Questions:
Title: Duplicate browser tab ignores form elements current values.
Body: <h2>Issue</h2>
<p>When you duplicate a tab in your browser, the current values of <code>form</code> elements are ignored. Tested in the latest versions of Chrome, Firefox and Edge on a Windows 11 computer.</p>
<h2>Sample code &amp; demo</h2>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var textarea = document.querySelector('textarea');
var p = document.querySelector('p');
var range = document.querySelector('input[type="range"]');
var output = document.querySelector('output');
var checkbox = document.querySelector('input[type="checkbox"]');
var span = document.querySelector('span');
var theme = document.querySelector('select');

function write() {
  p.textContent = textarea.value;
  output.value = range.value;
  span.textContent = checkbox.checked;
  document.body.className = theme.value;
}

textarea.addEventListener('input', write);
range.addEventListener('input', write);
checkbox.addEventListener('change', write);
theme.addEventListener('change', write);

write();</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>body {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, max-content);
  gap: 1em 0.5em;
}

body.Dark {
  color: white;
  background: black;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;textarea&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/textarea&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;input type="range"&gt;
&lt;output&gt;&lt;/output&gt;
&lt;input type="checkbox"&gt;
&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;select&gt;
  &lt;option&gt;Light&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option&gt;Dark&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p><strong><a href="https://labground.github.io/duplicate-browser-tab-ignores-form-elements-current-values.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DEMO</a></strong></p>
<h2>Steps to reproduce the issue</h2>
<ol>
<li>Open the <a href="https://labground.github.io/duplicate-browser-tab-ignores-form-elements-current-values.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">demo page</a> or create your own.</li>
<li>Change the <code>textarea</code>, <code>input</code>s, and <code>select</code> default values.</li>
<li>Duplicate the tab.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/nn3hK.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/nn3hK.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<ol start="4">
<li>The <code>p</code>, <code>output</code> and <code>span</code> elements don't show the expected text content and the theme is still light.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Question</h2>
<ol>
<li>Why does it happen?</li>
<li>What's the solution?</li>
</ol>

Answers:
Certainly! The behavior you're experiencing is due to how browsers handle form elements when duplicating tabs. When you duplicate a tab, the initial state of the form elements is preserved, but subsequent changes made in the new tab are not reflected in the original tab. This can lead to inconsistencies if you rely on the form elements' values across tabs.

### Explanation
When you duplicate a tab, the browser creates a new instance of the tab with the same initial state. Subsequent changes made in the new tab are stored separately from the original tab. This is why you see the expected values in the new tab but not in the original tab.

### Solution
To ensure that the form elements' values are consistent across tabs, you can implement a mechanism to synchronize the values between the tabs. Here’s how you can do it using JavaScript:

1. **Store Values**: Store the values of the form elements in a global variable or an array.
2. **Update Values**: When a form element changes, update the global variable or array.
3. **Sync Values**: When duplicating a tab, restore the values from the global variable or array.

Here’s an example implementation:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Duplicate Browser Tab Ignores Form Elements</title>
    <style>
        body {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(2, max-content);
            gap: 1em 0.5em;
        }
        body.Dark {
            color: white;
            background: black;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <textarea id="textarea" onchange="write()"></textarea>
    <p id="p"></p>
    <input type="range" id="range" oninput="write()" />
    <output id="output"></output>
    <input type="checkbox" id="checkbox" onchange="write()" />
    <span id="span"></span>
    <select id="theme" onchange="write()">
        <option value="Light">Light</option>
        <option value="Dark">Dark</option>
    </select>

    <script>
        let formValues = {
            textarea: '',
            range: 0,
            checkbox: false,
            theme: 'Light'
        };

        function write() {
            formValues.textarea = document.getElementById('textarea').value;
            formValues.range = document.getElementById('range').value;
            formValues.checkbox = document.getElementById('checkbox').checked;
            formValues.theme = document.getElementById('theme').value;

            document.getElementById('p').textContent = formValues.textarea;
            document.getElementById('output').value = formValues.range;
            document.getElementById('span').textContent = formValues.checkbox;
            document.body.className = formValues.theme;
        }

        write();

        // Sync values on tab duplication
        window.addEventListener('load', () => {
            if (window.location.hash.startsWith('#tab')) {
                const tabId = window.location.hash.substring(1);
                const tabData = localStorage.getItem(`tab-${tabId}`);
                if (tabData) {
                    const parsedData = JSON.parse(tabData);
                    for (const key in parsedData) {
                        if (parsedData.hasOwnProperty(key)) {
                            document.getElementById(key).value = parsedData[key];
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        });

        // Save values on tab duplication
        window.addEventListener('beforeunload', () => {
            const tabId = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100000);
            localStorage.setItem(`tab-${tabId}`, JSON.stringify(formValues));
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation of the Solution
1. **Form Values Storage**: The `formValues` object stores the values of the form elements.
2. **Write Function**: The `write` function updates the `formValues` object and synchronizes the values with the DOM elements.
3. **Tab Duplication**: On tab duplication, the values are retrieved from local storage and applied to the form elements.

This approach ensures that the form elements' values are consistent across tabs.

