Title: Add a new key and value to a Javascript object from an existing array of values.
Body: <p>I have an existing array of ids that I'm trying to iterate over to add each as an <code>id:</code> key to an existing array of objects. I have tried a number of different loops (for, for in, map, forEach), but I keep having the same outcome - it only adds the first id to each object, so <code>id: 'a'</code> x 6</p>
<p>An example of what I have</p>
<pre><code>const ids = ['a','b','c','d','e','f']

const objArr = [
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
]

</code></pre>
<p>An example of what I want to achieve</p>
<pre><code>const objArr = [
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'a'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'b'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'c'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'd'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'e'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'f'
  }
]
</code></pre>
<p>Here is an example of a forEach loop with a nested for in loop which I have tried to no avail.</p>
<pre><code>ids.forEach((item) =&gt; {
    for (const key in objArr) {
      objArr[key].id = item
    }
  })
</code></pre>
<p>Can anyone explain what I'm doing wrong and how to achieve what I'm trying to do?</p>

Answers:
Certainly! It looks like you're trying to add a new key-value pair (`id`) to each object in your `objArr` array based on the corresponding values in your `ids` array. The issue with your current approach is that the `forEach` loop iterates over each element in `ids`, but it does not iterate over each object in `objArr`. Instead, it modifies the `id` property of the first object in `objArr` for all iterations of `ids`.

Here’s how you can achieve your desired result using the `map` function, which is a more straightforward way to transform arrays:

```javascript
const ids = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f'];

const objArr = [
  {
    property: "some value"
  },
  {
    property: "some value"
  },
  {
    property: "some value"
  },
  {
    property: "some value"
  },
  {
    property: "some value"
  },
  {
    property: "some value"
  }
];

// Use map to create a new array with updated objects
const updatedObjArr = ids.map((id, index) => ({
  ...objArr[index],
  id: id
}));

console.log(updatedObjArr);
```

### Explanation:
1. **Map Function**: The `map` function creates a new array populated with the results of calling a provided function on every element in the calling array.
2. **Spread Operator (`...`)**: This is used to spread the properties of the original object into the new object being created.
3. **Index Parameter**: The second parameter to `map` is the index of the current element being processed. This allows you to access the corresponding object in `objArr`.

This will produce the desired output:

```javascript
[
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: 'a'
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: 'b'
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: 'c'
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: 'd'
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: 'e'
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: 'f'
  }
]
```

This approach ensures that each object in `objArr` gets its corresponding `id` from the `ids` array without modifying any other properties unintentionally.

