```python
awk -v cols=3 '{
    n = split($0, a, ";");
    for (i=1; i<=n; i++) {
        printf "%s", a[i];
        if (i<cols) printf ";";
        else printf "\n";
    }
}' input.txt > output.txt
```
This code splits the input file `input.txt` into multiple columns, with `cols` set to 3. It prints each column separated by a semicolon, and a newline character at the end of each line.
The `split` function splits the current line into an array `a`, with the delimiter set to `;`. The `n` variable stores the number of elements in the array.
The `for` loop iterates over the array, printing each element separated by a semicolon, and a newline character at the end of each line.
The `printf` function is used to print the elements of the array, with the `;` and `\n` characters inserted at the appropriate places.
The output is redirected to `output.txt`, which will contain the desired multiple columns. You can change the `cols` variable to adjust the number of columns.
This code should work even if the input file has an uneven number of lines. The code will automatically split the last line into the appropriate number of columns, and will add empty columns as needed to match the specified number of columns.
