Title: Miller: ofs=&quot;\n&quot;?.
Body: <p>Suppose I have:</p>
<pre><code>cat file
&quot;Field 1, Line 1&quot;,Field 2,&quot;Field 3, Line 1&quot;
&quot;Field 1, Line 2&quot;,Field 2,&quot;Field 3, Line 2&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>With Miller, I want to produce:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;Field 1, Line 1&quot;
&quot;Field 2&quot;
&quot;Field 3, Line 1&quot;
&quot;Field 1, Line 2&quot;
&quot;Field 2&quot;
&quot;Field 3, Line 2&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>I tried this:</p>
<pre><code>mlr --csv -N  --ofs lf --quote-all cat file
</code></pre>
<p>Which produces no output.</p>
<p>As a workaround, I can do this:</p>
<pre><code>mlr --csv -N  --ofs pipe --quote-all cat file | sed 's/&quot;\|&quot;/&quot;\n&quot;/g'
</code></pre>
<p>Or use ruby:</p>
<pre><code>ruby -r csv -e 'CSV.parse($&lt;.read).flatten.each{|r| puts [r].to_csv(force_quotes: true)}' file
</code></pre>
<p>But it does feel that I should be able to just look at a csv file one field at a time (and ignore records) with Miller?</p>

Answers:
It appears that you have encountered an issue with the <code>deepcopy</code> function when using the <code>Counter</code> class from the <code>collections</code> module. This issue arises because the <code>Counter</code> class does not have a custom <code>__deepcopy__</code> method, and as a result, the <code>deepcopy</code> function does not create a deep copy of the <code>Counter</code> instance.

To resolve this issue, you can define a custom <code>__deepcopy__</code> method in your <code>MyCounter</code> class. This method will ensure that the deep copy is created correctly. Here's an example of how you can implement the custom <code>__deepcopy__</code> method:

<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy
from collections import Counter

class MyCounter(Counter):
    def __init__(self, foo):
        self.foo = foo

    def __deepcopy__(self, memo):
        return MyCounter(self.foo)
</code></pre>

With this custom <code>__deepcopy__</code> method, the <code>deepcopy</code> function will create a deep copy of your <code>MyCounter</code> instances, and the <code>foo</code> attribute will be copied correctly.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.

Questions:
Title: Dictionary seems to keep inserted order.
Body: <p>I’ve always assumed that a <code>Dictionary</code> does not preserve the order of which items are added.
Then why does this always seem to be true?</p>
<pre><code>internal class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var d = new Dictionary&lt;int, int&gt;();
        var l = new List&lt;int&gt;();
        var r = new Random();

        for (int i = 1; i &lt; 100_000; i++)
        {
            var j = r.Next();
            if (!d.ContainsKey(j))
            {
                d.Add(j, 1);
                l.Add(j);
            }
        }

        Console.WriteLine(d.Select(kvp =&gt; kvp.Key).SequenceEqual(l));
    }
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
None. There's no way to add an element to a .NET Dictionary without checking if the key already exists. The ContainsKey() method is used to ensure that the key is unique and that the value is not overwritten. If the key already exists, the value is updated instead of being added to the dictionary. So, there is no way to add an element to a .NET Dictionary without checking if the key already exists. However, you can use the TryAdd() method in .NET Core 2.0 to improve performance by checking only once if the dictionary contains the key before adding the element. If you want to lazy build the value, you can use a separate method to build the value and then add it to the dictionary only if the key does not already exist. Here's an example:</p>
<pre><code>public void AddIfNotExists(Dictionary&lt;string, Value&gt; dico, string key)
{
    if (!dico.ContainsKey(key))
    {
        dico.Add(key, BuildValue());
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Replace BuildValue() with your own method that builds the value. This method will be called only if the key does not already exist in the dictionary. This way, you can achieve both checking for the key and lazy building the value without calling ContainsKey() twice.

