The behavior you are observing is due to the fact that <code>IEnumerable.Contains</code> is implemented differently for different types of collections. For <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Contains</code> is implemented as a linear search, which works fine with nullable types. However, for <code>KeyCollection</code> of a <code>Dictionary</code>, <code>Contains</code> is implemented as a binary search, which throws an <code>ArgumentNullException</code> if null is passed in.

This is why you get different behavior when calling <code>Contains</code> on a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> and a <code>KeyCollection</code> of a <code>Dictionary</code>. To avoid this issue, you can convert the <code>KeyCollection</code> to a list before treating them as <code>IEnumerable</code>. Here is an example:

<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>IEnumerable&lt;string&gt; x = new Dictionary&lt;string, string&gt;().Keys.ToList();
Console.WriteLine(x.Contains(null));
</code></pre>

Alternatively, you can also use the <code>Any</code> method instead of <code>Contains</code> to check if a null value exists in the collection. The <code>Any</code> method returns true if the collection contains at least one element that satisfies a specified condition, which in this case is checking if the element is null.

<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>IEnumerable&lt;string&gt; x = new Dictionary&lt;string, string&gt;().Keys;
Console.WriteLine(x.Any(e => e == null));
</code></pre>

This will return false in both cases, regardless of whether the collection is a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> or a <code>KeyCollection</code> of a <code>Dictionary</code>. This is because the <code>Any</code> method is designed to handle nullable types and will not throw an exception.<|fim_middle|>

Questions:
Title: Resharper inspection issue &quot;Redundant dictionary &#39;ContainsKey&#39; before adding to the collection&quot;.
Body: <p>Resharper is throwing this inspection issue:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Redundant dictionary 'ContainsKey' before adding to the collection</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At this part of the code:</p>
<pre><code>var userPref = new Preferences { Key = key, Value = value };

if (this.preferencesDictionary.ContainsKey(key))
{
    this.preferencesDictionary[key] = userPref;
}
else
{
    this.preferencesDictionary.Add(key, userPref);
}

return this;
</code></pre>
<p>I don't really think this is redundant. It is an error that I should skip? Or is there really an improvement here?</p>

Answers:
None. This is not a redundant check. It is correct. Resharper is suggesting that you use <code>TryAdd</code> instead of <code>ContainsKey</code> and <code>Add</code> to avoid the redundant check. This is a good practice to avoid unnecessary checks and improve performance. Here is the updated code:</p>
