The Application Support directory is a common location on iOS where your app can store persistent data that should not be deleted when the app is uninstalled. It is typically located at `/Library/Application Support/<YourAppName>/`.

On iOS 17, the structure of the Application Support directory may have changed slightly. However, it should still be accessible through the `applicationSupportDirectory` property of `FileManager`.

If you are unable to access the Application Support directory using the `applicationSupportDirectory` property, it may be due to changes in how the directory is accessed or stored on iOS 17. You can try using the `URL(forResource:withExtension:inDirectory:)` method of `FileManager` to create a URL for the Application Support directory and then read the contents of that directory.

Here's an example of how you can access the contents of the Application Support directory:

```swift
let fileManager = FileManager.default
let appSupportDirectory = fileManager.urls(for: .applicationSupportDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!

// Get the path to the Application Support directory
let appSupportPath = appSupportDirectory.path

// Create a URL for the Application Support directory
let appSupportURL = URL(fileURLWithPath: appSupportPath)

// Read the contents of the Application Support directory
do {
    let contents = try fileManager.contentsOfDirectory(at: appSupportURL, includingPropertiesForKeys: nil, options: [])
    for item in contents {
        print(item.path)
    }
} catch {
    print("Error reading contents of Application Support directory: \(error)")
}
```

This code creates a URL for the Application Support directory and then reads the contents of that directory using the `contentsOfDirectory(at:includingPropertiesForKeys:options:)` method of `FileManager`.

Note that the `applicationSupportDirectory` property may return a different path on different devices or versions of iOS, so it's important to use the `URL(forResource:withExtension:inDirectory:)` method to create a URL for the Application Support directory and then read the contents of that directory.<|fim_middle|>

Questions:
Title: Why does a C style &#39;class&#39; result in extra instructions?.
Body: <p>Comparing C style struct with 'member' functions to a C++ class in an attempt to model C++ overheads, I suspected that the following implementation would be roughly equivlent by containing the same number of instructions. I see that the C implementation results in extra instuctions when calling the 'member' functions.</p>
<pre><code>int main()
{
    uint32_t a;
    rectangle_t r;

    /* struct */
    r.set(2,3, &amp;r);
    /* asm 
       ldr     r3, [r7, #12]
       adds    r2, r7, #4
       movs    r1, #3
       movs    r0, #2
       blx     r3
    */

    a = r.getArea(&amp;r);
    /* asm 
       ldr     r3, [r7, #16]
       adds    r2, r7, #4
       mov     r0, r2
       blx     r3
       str     r0, [r7, #20]
       movs    r3, #0
    */

    /* Class */
    r.set(2, 3);
    /* asm 
       adds    r3, r7, #4
       movs    r2, #3
       movs    r1, #2
       mov     r0, r3
       bl      rectangle_t::set(unsigned int, unsigned int)
    */
    a = r.getArea();
    /* asm 
       adds    r3, r7, #4
       mov     r0, r3
       bl      rectangle_t::getArea()
       str     r0, [r7, #12]
       movs    r3, #0
    */
}
</code></pre>
<p>Why is there an extra ldr instruction when calling a stuct 'member' vs a class member?</p>
<p>Compiler: ARM GCC 12.2.0 (linux)</p>
<p>Declarations:</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct rectangle rectangle_t;
struct rectangle
{
    uint32_t w;
    uint32_t l;
    void (*set)(uint32_t L, uint32_t W, rectangle_t *self);
    uint32_t (*getArea)(rectangle_t *self);    
};
void set(uint32_t L, uint32_t W, rectangle_t *self)
{
    self-&gt;w = W;
    self-&gt;l = L;
}

uint32_t getArea(rectangle_t *self)
{
    return self-&gt;l * self-&gt;w;
}

class rectangle_t
{
public:   

    uint32_t w;
    uint32_t l;  
    void set(uint32_t L, uint32_t W);
    uint32_t getArea(); 
};

void rectangle_t::set(uint32_t L, uint32_t W)
{
    w = W;
    l = L;
}

uint32_t rectangle_t::getArea()
{
    return l * w;
}
</code></pre>

Title: Why is a private member visible when initializing a static variable?.
Body: <p>This compiles:</p>
<pre><code>class Node{
    int m = 0;

    static unsigned f(){
        return 1;
    }

public:
    static unsigned a;
    static unsigned b;
};

unsigned Node::a = sizeof(m); // &lt;= !!!
unsigned Node::b = f();       // &lt;= !!!

int main(){
}
</code></pre>
<p>Why? I know <code>sizeof</code> is not function but still, aren't <code>m</code> and <code>f()</code> private?</p>
<p>It is definitely not a bug, because the code compiles on GCC, MSVC, Clang and Intel C++.</p>
<p>Update: Also, note that it is just <code>sizeof(m)</code> and not <code>sizeof(Node::m)</code>; same with <code>f()</code> instead of <code>Node::f()</code>. (Koenig's lookup?)</p>

Title: same method or variable name in a javascript class behaves differently.
Body: <pre><code>class Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Parent&quot;);
    }
}
class Child extends Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Parent&quot;);
    }
}
var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method);
</code></pre>
<p>console return method in child class which is a expected behaviour.</p>
<pre><code>class Parent{
    method = &quot;sss&quot;
}
class Child extends Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Child&quot;)
    }
}
var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method)
</code></pre>
<p>why does the console return method variable - &quot;sss&quot; in Parent class ?</p>

Answers:
In JavaScript, the behavior you're observing is due to how class inheritance works and how JavaScript handles property assignment. Let's break it down step by step:

1. **Method Overriding in JavaScript:**
   When you define a method in a class and then override it in a subclass, the method in the subclass is used instead of the one in the superclass. This is a common feature in object-oriented programming languages.

   ```javascript
   class Parent {
     method() {
       console.log("Parent");
     }
   }

   class Child extends Parent {
     method() {
       console.log("Child");
     }
   }

   var child = new Child();
   console.log(child.method); // This will log "Child"
   ```

2. **Property Assignment:**
   When you use the `=` operator to assign a value to a property within a class, it creates a static property on the prototype of the class. Static properties are shared among all instances of the class and are not instance-specific.

   ```javascript
   class Parent {
     method = "sss";
   }

   class Child extends Parent {
     method() {
       console.log("Child");
     }
   }

   var child = new Child();
   console.log(child.method); // This will log "sss"
   ```

   In this case, `method` is a static property defined on the `Parent` class's prototype. This means that every instance of `Parent` (including `Child`) will share the same `method` property.

3. **Why `console.log(child.method)` Returns `"sss"`?**
   When you call `console.log(child.method)`, it is logging the value of the `method` property, which is `"sss"` because it is a static property defined on the `Parent` class's prototype. This is why you see `"sss"` being logged, even though you're trying to access the method of the `Child` class.

To illustrate this further, consider the following example:

```javascript
class Parent {
  static method = "sss";
}

class Child extends Parent {
  method() {
    console.log("Child");
  }
}

var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method); // This will log "sss"
```

In this example, `method` is a static property of the `Parent` class, and it is being accessed through the `Child` instance (`child`), but it still returns `"sss"` because it is a static property.

If you want to ensure that the method of the `Child` class is used, you should override the method in the `Child` class:

```javascript
class Parent {
  method() {
    console.log("Parent");
  }
}

class Child extends Parent {
  method() {
    console.log("Child");
  }
}

var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method); // This will log "Child"
```

This way, the method of the `Child` class will be called when you access `child.method`.

Questions:
Title: Why does the `instanceof` operator return false on instance passed to library? (No inheritance involved).
Body: <p>I am building a library with TS. This library uses the <code>ssh2</code> library as a dependency.</p>
<p>I'm trying to create a function that can either accept an <code>ssh2</code> configuration object or an already existing <code>Client</code> instance to execute a command (this is a simplified case):</p>
<pre><code>import { Client, ConnectConfig } from 'ssh2';

export function runCommand(params: Client | ConnectConfig) {
  if(params instanceof Client) {
    // do something
  } else {
    // create our own client
    // do something
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>When I build this library and call the function like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const { readFileSync } = require(&quot;fs&quot;);
const { Client } = require(&quot;ssh2&quot;);
const { runCommand } = require(&quot;myLib&quot;);
const conn = new Client();

conn
  .on(&quot;ready&quot;, () =&gt; {
    console.log(&quot;Client :: ready&quot;);
    runCommand(conn);
  })
  .connect({
    host: &quot;example.com&quot;,
    port: 22,
    username: &quot;me&quot;,
    privateKey: readFileSync(process.env.HOME + &quot;/.ssh/id_ed25519&quot;),
  });
</code></pre>
<p>the <code>instanceof</code> check in my function will return <code>false</code> for some reason. What exactly is happening here? Is it a TS compilation issue or does Node.js see the <code>Client</code> from my lib's dependency and my consumer code's dependency as two different classes?</p>
<p>The compiled code looks something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const ssh2_1 = require(&quot;ssh2&quot;);
//...
function runCommand(params) {
    if (params instanceof ssh2_1.Client) {
        // do something
    }
    else {
       // create our own client
    // do something
    }
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
The `instanceof` operator in JavaScript checks if an object is an instance of a specific class or constructor. In your case, the `instanceof` operator is returning `false` because the `params` object is not an instance of the `Client` class from the `ssh2` library. This is likely due to the fact that the `Client` class from the `ssh2` library is not the same as the `Client` class from your library's dependency. The `instanceof` operator checks for reference equality, and since the two `Client` classes are not the same object, the `instanceof` operator returns `false`.
If you want to check if the `params` object is an instance of the `Client` class from your library's dependency, you can do so by checking the constructor of the `params` object. Here's an example:
<pre><code>import { Client } from 'ssh2';

export function runCommand(params: Client | ConnectConfig) {
  if (params.constructor.name === 'Client') {
    // do something
  } else {
    // create our own client
    // do something
  }
}
</code></pre>
This will check if the `constructor` property of the `params` object is equal to the `constructor` property of the `Client` class from your library's dependency, which should return `true` if the `params` object is an instance of the `Client` class from your library's dependency.

