Title: Java DateTimeFormatter Not Working for Pattern &#39;ddMyyyy&#39;.
Body: <p>Consider the following date:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String date = &quot;2391995&quot;;
String patt = &quot;ddMyyyy&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>Using the pattern provided, we can see that <code>date</code> represents September 23, 1995.</p>
<p>But consider what happens when trying to parse it - first the old way, and then the Java 8+ way:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Date dd = new SimpleDateFormat(patt).parse(date);
System.out.println(dd);

DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(patt);
LocalDate d = LocalDate.parse(date, formatter);
System.out.println(d);
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Sat Sep 23 00:00:00 EDT 1995
Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.time.format.DateTimeParseException: Text '2391995' could not be parsed at index 7
    ...
</code></pre>
<p>As we can see, the old way, using <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> works as intended. But when using <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> and <code>LocalDate.parse</code>, an error occurs. Why is this?</p>

Answers:
In Java, the <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> class is a flexible way to format and parse dates. It uses a set of predefined constants and patterns to parse and format dates. On the other hand, <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> is part of the Java 8+ date and time API and is designed to parse and format dates using a pattern string.

The pattern string &#39;ddMyyyy&#39; you provided is not a valid pattern for <code>DateTimeFormatter</code>. The pattern string must follow the ISO-8601 standard, which uses &#39;dd&#39; for day, &#39;MM&#39; for month, and &#39;yyyy&#39; for year.

Here is an example of how to use <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> with the correct pattern:

<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.time.format.DateTimeParseException;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String date = "2391995";
        String patt = "ddMMyyyy";

        try {
            LocalDate d = LocalDate.parse(date, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(patt));
            System.out.println(d);
        } catch (DateTimeParseException e) {
            System.out.println("Error parsing date: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

In this example, we use <code>DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(patt)</code> to create a <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> object with the correct pattern. We then use <code>LocalDate.parse</code> to parse the date string using the formatter. If the pattern is not valid, an exception is thrown and caught in the <code>catch</code> block.

Note that <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> is a more flexible and powerful way to parse and format dates compared to <code>SimpleDateFormat</code>. It is recommended to use <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> instead of <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> for new code.

Questions:
Title: Get past plus future date and get all dates in said range.
Body: <p>I am using Howard Hinnant's awesome <a href="https://howardhinnant.github.io/date/date.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">date</a> library to do some date calculations.</p>
<p>What I want to do is get Monday's date wherever I am and then get Sunday's date wherever I am. Then I want to get all the dates in between said Monday and Sunday. I don't need time info, just the dates</p>
<p>With this <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/56505034/how-to-find-the-date-of-the-previous-monday-in-c">answer</a>, I am able to get Monday's date as per Howard Hinnant's answer.</p>
<p>What I am struggling with is going <em>forward</em> to calculate Sunday's and thus I'm not sure how to get all the dates between the ranges (Monday - Sunday)</p>
<p>This what I have tried thus far:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

#include &lt;date/date.h&gt;

int main()
{
    auto currentDateYMD = date::year_month_day{ date::floor&lt;date::days&gt;(std::chrono::system_clock::now()) };
    auto todays_date = date::floor&lt;date::days&gt;(std::chrono::system_clock::now());
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Current Date: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, currentDateYMD) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;;

    date::year_month_day mondayDate = todays_date - (date::weekday{ todays_date } - date::Monday);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Monday: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, mondayDate) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;;

    todays_date = date::floor&lt;date::days&gt;(std::chrono::system_clock::now());

    todays_date += date::days{ date::Sunday.c_encoding() }; // should go forward to Sunday's date
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Sunday: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, todays_date) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;; // prints today's date

    // calculate range
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i &lt; /*date::Sunday.c_encoding()*/ 7; i++) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Future Date: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, ++todays_date) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;;
    }

    std::cin.get();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Which outputs this:</p>
<pre><code>Current Date: 2023-09-04
Monday: 2023-09-04
Sunday: 2023-09-04
Future Date: 2023-09-05
Future Date: 2023-09-06
Future Date: 2023-09-07
Future Date: 2023-09-08
Future Date: 2023-09-09
Future Date: 2023-09-10
Future Date: 2023-09-11
</code></pre>
<p>I am not sure what I am missing nor am I sure how to &quot;traverse the range&quot; to get dates</p>
<p>I am using C++17 thus I am using the <code>date</code> library and not <code>std::chrono</code>.</p>

Answers:
None

Here's a modified version of your code that should give you the desired output:

```cpp
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <date/date.h>

int main()
{
    auto currentDateYMD = date::year_month_day{ date::floor<std::chrono::days>(std::chrono::system_clock::now()) };
    auto todays_date = date::floor<std::chrono::days>(std::chrono::system_clock::now());
    std::cout << "Current Date: " << date::format("%F", currentDateYMD) << "\n";

    date::year_month_day mondayDate = todays_date - (date::weekday{ todays_date } - date::Monday);
    std::cout << "Monday: " << date::format("%F", mondayDate) << "\n";

    todays_date = date::floor<std::chrono::days>(std::chrono::system_clock::now());

    // Go forward to Sunday's date
    date::year_month_day sundayDate = mondayDate + date::days{ 6 };
    std::cout << "Sunday: " << date::format("%F", sundayDate) << "\n";

    // Calculate range
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < 7; i++) {
        std::cout << "Date: " << date::format("%F", mondayDate + date::days{i}) << "\n";
    }

    std::cin.get();
    return 0;
}
```

In this modified version, we first calculate the Sunday's date by adding 6 days to the Monday's date. Then, we use a for loop to iterate over the range of dates between Monday and Sunday, printing each date in the desired format.

The output of this code should be:

```
Current Date: 2023-09-04
Monday: 2023-09-04
Sunday: 2023-09-10
Date: 2023-09-04
Date: 2023-09-05
Date: 2023-09-06
Date: 2023-09-07
Date: 2023-09-08
Date: 2023-09-09
Date: 2023-09-10
```

This should give you all the dates in the range between Monday and Sunday.
