Answers:
Certainly, I can help you resolve this issue. It looks like you're using Next.js 13 and NextAuth.js, and you're trying to implement a login page. Here's how you can fix the issues you're facing:

1. First, make sure you have the necessary dependencies installed. You should have `next-auth` and `@next-auth/[provider]` installed. For example, if you're using Google authentication, you would have `next-auth` and `@next-auth/google` installed.

2. In your `layout.tsx` file, you need to wrap your entire application with a `SessionProvider` component. This component is provided by NextAuth.js and allows you to access the user session data in your components.

<pre><code>import Image from 'next/image'
import { SessionProvider } from "next-auth/react";
import { ReactNode } from "react";
import React from 'react';

import "./globals.css";
import Link from "next/link";

type Props = {
  children: ReactNode;
  session: any;
};

export default function Layout({ children, session }: Props) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <head />
      <body>
        <div className="mx-auto">
          <div className="flex flex-row flex-wrap">
            <aside className="w-full sm:w-1/3 md:w-1/5 xl:w-1/6 border-r-2 border-[#F845C6]">
              <div className="sticky top-0 p-4 w-full">
                <ul className="flex flex-col overflow-hidden">
                  <li className="m-5 text-center">
                    <Link href="/login">LOGO</Link>
                  </li>
                  <li className="m-5 text-center">
                    <Link href="/">Dashboard</Link>
                  </li>
                  <li className="m-5 text-center">
                    <Link href="tasks">Tasks</Link>
                  </li>
                  <li className="m-5 text-center">
                    <Link href="reporting">Reporting</Link>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </aside>

            <div className="w-full sm:w-2/3 md:w-4/5 xl:w-5/6">
              {children}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}

export function getLayout(page: any) {
  const { session } = page.props;

  return (
    <SessionProvider session={session}>
      <Layout session={session}>{page}</Layout>
    </SessionProvider>
  );
}
</code></pre>

3. Next, in your `login.tsx` file, you need to import the `useSession` hook from `next-auth/react` and use it to access the user session data.

<pre><code>&quot;use client&quot;;
import React from 'react'
import { useSession, signIn, signOut } from 'next-auth/react'

export default function Login() {
  const { data: session } = useSession();

  if (session) {
    return (
      &lt;div style={{ fontFamily: 'sans-serif', margin: '10px 20px' }}&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;Welcome, {session.user.name}&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;img src={session.user.image} alt=&quot;&quot; style={{ borderRadius: '15px', width: '80px', margin: '10px' }} /&gt;
        &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; signOut()}&gt;Sign out&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    );
  } else {
    return (
      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;Please sign in&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; signIn(&quot;google&quot;, { callbackUrl: '/account' })}&gt;Sign in with Google&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    );
  }
}
</code></pre>

4. Finally, make sure you have the `signIn` and `signOut` functions imported from `next-auth/react` in your `login.tsx` file.

<pre><code>import { useSession, signIn, signOut } from 'next-auth/react'
</code></pre>

With these changes, you should be able to resolve the issues you're facing and have a working login page in your Next.js 13 app. Let me know if you have any further questions!

