Answers:
It appears that the `ProviderSettings` class has been deprecated in favor of `ClientSettings` and `AuthorizationServerSettings` in the latest version of Spring Security OAuth2 Authorization Server. You can use the `ClientSettings` class instead of `ProviderSettings` in your code. Here's an updated version of your code:

```java
@Bean
public ClientSettings clientSettings() {
    return ClientSettings.builder()
            .authorizationGrantTypes(
                    AuthorizationGrantType.values())
            .redirectUris("http://localhost:9000/login/oauth2/code/my-client")
            .build();
}
```

You can also use the `AuthorizationServerSettings` class if you need to configure additional settings for the authorization server.

It's worth noting that the `spring-security-oauth2-authorization-server` dependency version you're using (1.0.1) is quite old. You may want to consider updating to a newer version of Spring Security OAuth2 Authorization Server to ensure compatibility with the latest features and bug fixes.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.

You are correct in assuming that you need to set the headers and cookies on the client-side code. However, you can use the `headers()` and `cookies()` functions to read the values of the headers and cookies on the server-side.
To set the headers and cookies on the client-side, you can use the `useEffect()` hook to call a function that sets the headers and cookies. Here is an example of how you can do this:
```jsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';

function MyComponent() {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Set the headers and cookies here
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Your component code here */}
    </div>
  );
}
```
In the `useEffect()` hook, you can call a function that sets the headers and cookies using the `headers()` and `cookies()` functions. Here is an example of how you can do this:
```javascript
import { useEffect } from 'react';

function MyComponent() {
  useEffect(() => {
    const setHeadersAndCookies = () => {
      // Set the headers and cookies here
      const headers = new Headers();
      headers.append('Authorization', 'Bearer ' + token);
      const cookies = new Cookies();
      cookies.set('my-cookie', token);
      // Set the headers and cookies in the response
      response.headers = headers;
      response.cookies = cookies;
    };

    // Call the function to set the headers and cookies
    setHeadersAndCookies();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Your component code here */}
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, the `setHeadersAndCookies()` function sets the `Authorization` header with the value of the `token` variable, and sets the `my-cookie` cookie with the value of the `token` variable. The `headers()` and `cookies()` functions are used to create the headers and cookies, and the `set()` method is used to set the value of the cookie.
Once the headers and cookies are set, you can read them in the server component using the `headers()` and `cookies()` functions. Here is an example of how you can do this:
```python
import { headers, cookies } from 'next/headers';

function MyServerComponent() {
  const token = cookies().get('my-cookie').value;
  const headers = headers().get('Authorization');

  // Use the token and headers in your server component code

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Your server component code here */}
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, the `cookies()` and `headers()` functions are used to get the value of the `my-cookie` cookie and the `Authorization` header, respectively. The `value` property of the cookie is used to get the value of the cookie, and the `get()` method of the headers is used to get the value of the header.
I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions.
