I understand your concern. In Node.js, there are several ways to close an HTTP request and prevent further data from being sent over the wire. Here's a summary of the options you mentioned and some additional details:
1. Calling <code>response.end()</code>: This will close the response and the underlying connection, preventing further data from being sent over the wire. However, it will not close the request, so the client can still send more data.
2. Calling <code>request.destroy()</code>: This will close the request and the underlying connection, preventing further data from being sent over the wire. However, it will not close the response, so the client can still send more data.
3. Unpiping the request from any <code>Transform</code>s/<code>Writeable</code>s and calling <code>request.resume()</code>: This will prevent further data from being sent over the wire, but it will keep the server busy and keep the connection open.
4. Unpiping the request from any <code>Transform</code>s/<code>Writeable</code>s and calling <code>request.pause()</code>: This will prevent further data from being sent over the wire, but it will not close the request, so the client can still send more data.
To prevent the client from sending infinite amounts of data, you should call <code>request.destroy()</code> after you have closed the response. This will ensure that the request and the underlying connection are closed, preventing further data from being sent over the wire.
Here's an example of how you can implement this:
```
const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs');
const { Transform } = require('stream');
const fileUploadHandler = (request, response) => {
  // Check if the request size exceeds the limit
  if (request.headers['content-length'] > MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE) {
    response.statusCode = 413; // Request entity too large
    response.end('Request size exceeds the limit.');
    request.destroy();
    return;
  }
  // Create a transform stream to limit the request size
  const lengthLimiter = new Transform({
    transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
      const totalSize = this._totalSize + chunk.length;
      if (totalSize > MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE) {
        this.emit('error', new Error('Request size exceeds the limit.'));
      } else {
        this._totalSize = totalSize;
        this.push(chunk);
        callback();
      }
    },
    _totalSize: 0
  });
  // Unpipe the request from any Transform streams
  request.unpipe(lengthLimiter);
  // Pipe the request to the transform stream
  request.pipe(lengthLimiter);
  // Handle the request
  lengthLimiter.on('data', (chunk) => {
    // Handle the chunk
  });
  lengthLimiter.on('error', (error) => {
    // Handle the error
    response.statusCode = 413; // Request entity too large
    response.end('Request size exceeds the limit.');
    request.destroy();
  });
  lengthLimiter.on('end', () => {
    // Handle the end
    request.destroy();
  });
};
const server = http.createServer(fileUploadHandler);
server.listen(3000);
```
In this example, we create a <code>Transform</code> stream to limit the request size. We then unpipe the request from any <code>Transform</code> streams and pipe the request to the transform stream. We handle the request by listening to the <code>'data'</code>, <code>'error'</code>, and <code>'end'</code> events on the transform stream. If the request size exceeds the limit, we emit an error and destroy the request. This ensures that the request and the underlying connection are closed, preventing further data from being sent over the wire.

