To apply arbitrary validation to the SQLC-generated `Params` structures without re-creating the structure or modifying the tags, you can use the `validator` package in Go. This package provides a flexible way to validate data structures.

First, install the `validator` package by running the following command:

```
go get github.com/go-playground/validator/v10
```

Next, modify the `CreateUserBasicParams` struct to use the `validator` package. Here's an example:

```go
import (
    "github.com/go-playground/validator/v10"
)

type CreateUserBasicParams struct {
    Username string      `json:"username" validate:"required"`
    Email    string      `json:"email" validate:"required,email"`
    Phone    pgtype.Text `json:"phone" validate:"required"`
}

var validate *validator.Validate

func init() {
    validate = validator.New()
}

func (p CreateUserBasicParams) Validate() error {
    return validate.Struct(p)
}
```

In the above code, we import the `validator` package and define a `CreateUserBasicParams` struct with the required tags. We also initialize the `validate` variable to a new `validator.Validate` instance.

To use the `Validate()` method, you need to define a `Validate()` method on your struct. This method will return an error if any of the fields in the struct fail validation.

Now, in your Gin handler, you can call the `Validate()` method before binding the JSON data to the `CreateUserBasicParams` struct. Here's an example:

```go
func (c *Controller) Register(ctx *gin.Context) {
    var params CreateUserBasicParams
    if err := ctx.ShouldBindWith(&params, binding.JSON); err != nil {
        ctx.AbortWithError(http.StatusBadRequest, err)
    }
    if err := params.Validate(); err != nil {
        ctx.AbortWithError(http.StatusBadRequest, err)
    }
    log.Printf("Parameters:\n%+v", params)

    q := hmdb.New(c.DBPool)
    u, err := q.CreateUserBasic(ctx, params)
    if err != nil {
        ctx.AbortWithError(http.StatusBadRequest, err)
    }
    log.Printf("New User:\n%+v", u)

    ctx.JSON(http.StatusOK, gin.H{
        "message": "Good!",
    })
}
```

In the above code, we call the `Validate()` method on the `params` struct before binding the JSON data to it. If any of the fields fail validation, the `Validate()` method will return an error, which we then abort with the appropriate HTTP status code.

By using the `validator` package, you can apply arbitrary validation to the SQLC-generated `Params` structures without re-creating the structure or modifying the tags. This allows you to enforce validation rules for each field in the `Params` struct.

