To check if the date has a valid format in Yup, you can use the <code>format</code> test. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to only allow <code>dd.MM.yyyy</code> format:</p>
<pre><code>const dateToCheck = yup
    .date()
    .nullable()
    .transform((curr, orig) =&gt; (orig === '' ? null : curr))
    .required()
    .min(minDate, 'Date cannot be this early')
    .max(maxDate, 'Date too much in the future')
    .test('format', 'Date is invalid', date =&gt; {
        const dateStr = date?.toISOString().slice(0, 10);
        return /^[0-9]{2}\.[0-9]{2}\.[0-9]{4}$/.test(dateStr);
    });
</code></pre>
<p>This test will only allow dates with the format <code>dd.MM.yyyy</code> to pass validation. If the date format is invalid, Yup will throw an error with the message "Date is invalid."</p>
<p>You can modify the regular expression in the test to allow other formats as well. For example, to allow <code>yyyy/MM/dd</code> format, you can use the following regular expression:</p>
<pre><code>const dateStr = date?.toISOString().slice(0, 10);
return /^[0-9]{4}\/[0-9]{2}\/[0-9]{2}$/.test(dateStr);
</code></pre>
<p>However, keep in mind that this regular expression will only allow dates with the format <code>yyyy/MM/dd</code>. If you want to allow other formats, you may need to modify the regular expression accordingly.</p>
<p>It's worth noting that Yup's <code>date</code> validation only checks if the date is a valid date. It doesn't check if the date format is valid. If you want to check the date format as well, you may need to use a custom validation function instead of the <code>format</code> test.</p>

Questions:
Title: Customize error message in Nest js using class-validator.
Body: <p>I am working on a nest project, and using class-validator for validation.</p>
<p>Currently if there is any validation error, I am getting  error response as</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;statusCode&quot;: 400,
    &quot;message&quot;: [
        &quot;Title is too long. Maximal length is 50 characters, but actual is $value&quot;,
        &quot;Title is too short. Minimal length is 10 characters, but actual is $value&quot;
    ],
    &quot;error&quot;: &quot;Bad Request&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>But instead of message as array of string, can we have message as array of object. So, that FE can easy figure out which field is having the error, like</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;message&quot;: [
        { &quot;field&quot;: &quot;title&quot;, &quot;error&quot;: &quot;Title is too long. Maximal length is 50 characters, but actual is $value&quot; },
        { &quot;field&quot;: &quot;title&quot;, &quot;error&quot;: &quot;Title is too short. Minimal length is 10 characters, but actual is $value&quot; }
    ]
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
To customize the error message in Nest.js using class-validator, you can use the `@Transform` decorator to transform the validation errors into the desired format. Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates this:
<pre><code>import { Transform, TransformFnParams } from &quot;class-transformer&quot;;
import { IsString, Length } from &quot;class-validator&quot;;

export class MyDto {
  @Transform((params: TransformFnParams) => {
    if (params.error) {
      return {
        field: params.target.constructor.name,
        error: params.error.message,
      };
    }
    return params.value;
  })
  @IsString()
  @Length(10, 50)
  title: string;
}
</code></pre>
In this code, we define a DTO class `MyDto` with a `title` property. We use the `@Transform` decorator to transform the validation errors into the desired format. Inside the `@Transform` decorator, we check if there is an error using `params.error`. If there is an error, we return an object with the `field` property set to the name of the DTO class and the `error` property set to the error message. If there is no error, we simply return the original value.
By using this approach, you can customize the error message to include the field name and error message, making it easier for the frontend to handle validation errors.
