Questions:
Title: Add a new key and value to a Javascript object from an existing array of values.
Body: <p>I have an existing array of ids that I'm trying to iterate over to add each as an <code>id:</code> key to an existing array of objects. I have tried a number of different loops (for, for in, map, forEach), but I keep having the same outcome - it only adds the first id to each object, so <code>id: 'a'</code> x 6</p>
<p>An example of what I have</p>
<pre><code>const ids = ['a','b','c','d','e','f']

const objArr = [
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
  }
]

</code></pre>
<p>An example of what I want to achieve</p>
<pre><code>const objArr = [
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'a'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'b'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'c'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'd'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'e'
  }
  {
   property: &quot;some value&quot;,
   id: 'f'
  }
]
</code></pre>
<p>Here is an example of a forEach loop with a nested for in loop which I have tried to no avail.</p>
<pre><code>ids.forEach((item) =&gt; {
    for (const key in objArr) {
      objArr[key].id = item
    }
  })
</code></pre>
<p>Can anyone explain what I'm doing wrong and how to achieve what I'm trying to do?</p>

Answers:
To add a new key and value to each object in the `objArr` array, you can use the `map` method in combination with the `Object.assign` method. Here's the updated code:
<pre><code>const ids = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f']

const objArr = [
  {
    property: "some value",
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
  }
]

const newObjArr = ids.map((id) => {
  const newObj = Object.assign({}, objArr[0]) // create a copy of the first object in objArr
  newObj.id = id // add the new id key-value pair
  return newObj // return the new object
})

console.log(newObjArr)
</code></pre>
This will output:
<pre><code>[
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: "a"
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: "b"
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: "c"
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: "d"
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: "e"
  },
  {
    property: "some value",
    id: "f"
  }
]
</code></pre>
Let me know if you have any further questions!<|fim_middle|>

Questions:
Title: typescript type from string values from an object.
Body: <p>The object with values as array of strings:</p>
<pre><code>const STUFF = {
  abc: [&quot;string_1&quot;, &quot;string_2&quot;],
  def: [&quot;string_3&quot;, &quot;string_4&quot;],
  xyz: [&quot;string_1&quot;, &quot;string_2&quot;, &quot;string_4&quot;],
}
</code></pre>
<p>how to define a type that allows only values from these arrays?</p>
<pre><code>type StuffStr = &quot;string_1&quot; | &quot;string_2&quot; | &quot;string_3&quot; | &quot;string_4&quot;;
</code></pre>

Answers:
To define a type that allows only values from the arrays in the `STUFF` object, you can use a type union that includes all the possible values. Here's an example:
<pre><code>type StuffStr = &quot;string_1&quot; | &quot;string_2&quot; | &quot;string_3&quot; | &quot;string_4&quot;;
</code></pre>
This type `StuffStr` includes all the possible values from the arrays in the `STUFF` object. You can use this type in your code to ensure that only values from these arrays are allowed. For example, you can use it in a function parameter like this:
<pre><code>function processStuff(stuff: Record&lt;string, StuffStr[]&gt;): void {
  // process stuff
}
</code></pre>
In this example, the `processStuff` function takes an object `stuff` where the keys are strings and the values are arrays of `StuffStr` values. This ensures that only values from the arrays in the `STUFF` object can be used.

