Title: How can I return value(object) form filter method on Object.keys() instead of key?.
Body: <p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const notes = {
  'jk2334|notes-md-23': {
    id: 'notes-md-23',
    text: 'First Note'
  },
  'jk2334|notes-xd-34': {
    id: 'notes-xd-34',
    text: 'Second Note'
   },
   'fd4345|notes-mf-54': {
    id: 'notes-mf-54',
    text: 'Third Note'
   }
}

const result = Object.keys(notes).filter(note =&gt; {
        if(note.startsWith('jk2334')) {
            console.log('from inner -', notes[note]);
            return notes[note];
        }
    })

console.log(result)</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>If I run this code it returns only key but not the value of the object. But if I console inside the condition it returns the value.</p>
<p>I want to return the array of value not key. What should I do now?</p>

Answers:
You can use the `map()` method to transform the array of keys into an array of values. Here's an example:</p>
<pre class="language-python"><code>const notes = {
  'jk2334|notes-md-23': {
    id: 'notes-md-23',
    text: 'First Note'
  },
  'jk2334|notes-xd-34': {
    id: 'notes-xd-34',
    text: 'Second Note'
   },
   'fd4345|notes-mf-54': {
    id: 'notes-mf-54',
    text: 'Third Note'
   }
}

const result = Object.keys(notes).filter(note =&gt; {
        if(note.startsWith('jk2334')) {
            console.log('from inner -', notes[note]);
            return notes[note];
        }
    }).map(note =&gt; notes[note])

console.log(result)</code></pre>
<p>Explanation: The `map()` method iterates over the array of keys and for each key, it retrieves the corresponding value from the `notes` object using the `notes[note]` syntax. The resulting array of values is then logged to the console.</p>
<p>Hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.</p>
<|repo_name|>

