Title: Difference between map and map object in terraform.
Body: <p>I was looking through the difference between map and object. My understanding is as follows :</p>
<p>A map can contain any type as long as all the elements are of the same type</p>
<pre><code>variable &quot;project_defaults&quot; {
      type = map(string)
      default  = {
          project = &quot;example_project&quot;
          region = &quot;eu-west-1&quot; 
      }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>An object contains named attributes , each having their own type</p>
<pre><code>variable &quot;s3_buckets&quot; {
  type = object({
    name                   = string
    versioning             = bool
    s3_rules   = list(map(any))
  })
  description = &quot;List of maps for S3 buckets&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I have seen examples where  <code>map(object)</code> is used but am really unsure what the difference is compared to the type <code>object</code></p>
<pre><code>variable &quot;s3_buckets&quot; {
      type = map(object({
        name                   = string
        versioning             = bool
        s3_rules   = list(map(any))
      }))
      description = &quot;List of maps for S3 buckets&quot;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Am trying to make sense of when to make use of <code>map(object)</code> as opposed to using <code>object</code> . Syntax wise they look very similar but am unsure of the actual scenarios of when to use them.</p>

Answers:
In Terraform, a `map` and an `object` are both used to store collections of data, but they have different characteristics and use cases.

<p>A `map` is a collection of key-value pairs, where the keys are unique identifiers and the values can be of any type. The keys can be of any data type, but the values must be of the same type.</p>
<pre><code>variable &quot;project_defaults&quot; {
      type = map(string)
      default  = {
          project = &quot;example_project&quot;
          region = &quot;eu-west-1&quot; 
      }
    }
</code></pre>

<p>An `object` is a collection of named attributes, where each attribute has a specific type. The attributes are defined using the `type` keyword, and the values can be of any type.</p>
<pre><code>variable &quot;s3_buckets&quot; {
  type = object({
    name                   = string
    versioning             = bool
    s3_rules   = list(map(any))
  })
  description = &quot;List of maps for S3 buckets&quot;
}
</code></pre>

<p>The main difference between a `map` and an `object` is the way the data is structured and the way it is accessed. A `map` is accessed by using the key, while an `object` is accessed by using the attribute name.</p>
<pre><code>var.project_defaults[&quot;project&quot;] # accessing map using key
var.s3_buckets.name # accessing object using attribute name
</code></pre>

<p>When to use `map(object)` vs `object` depends on the use case. If you need to store a collection of objects with the same attributes, you can use `object`. If you need to store a collection of key-value pairs, you can use `map`. In some cases, you may need to use a combination of both, for example when storing a collection of objects with some attributes that are common to all objects and some attributes that are specific to each object.</p>

<p>It's important to note that the use of `map(object)` is not a common pattern in Terraform. It is generally recommended to use `object` when defining the structure of the data, and to use `map` when storing collections of objects with the same attributes.</p>
